body:
Worker trap (Termal::Opus3, 2026-10-29, my own slip in the Windows B pair-secret restore, w-cea20bdd4f38). The remote PowerShell granted `${env:USERDOMAIN}\${env:USERNAME}`. On a workgroup machine USERDOMAIN can be the workgroup name ("WG"), so icacls failed with "No mapping between account names and security IDs". icacls is a native exe, so `$ErrorActionPreference='Stop'` did NOT stop the script, and the secret was written into a file that still had its inherited ACL (including CodexSandboxUsers:RX) for about 30 s, until I fixed it.
RULE for any secret file on another machine:
(1) grant `"$env:COMPUTERNAME\$env:USERNAME"` (or the explicit `HOST\user`), never USERDOMAIN;
(2) check `$LASTEXITCODE` after icacls and throw on non-zero;
(3) read the ACL back and assert that it lists exactly the one principal BEFORE writing any secret byte;
(4) only then write.
